  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(10,8)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(9,6)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=2{X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N(2\\cdot 10+1\\cdot 9,{2}^{2}\\cdot 8+{1}^{2}\\cdot 6) \\\\  & ∼N(29,38).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $Y∼N(29,38).$",
